# The Android programs this branch measured, one APK each, and the Android SDK command-line tools
# they were run with. Four columns: the file's SHA-256; a name and the file, which is where
# `spikes/revocation/android/fetch.sh` puts it under the work directory's `dl`; the SHA-256 of the
# certificate that signed it; and where it is downloaded from. `fetch.sh` refuses a name with no
# line here and a file whose hash differs, and `verify-apks.sh` refuses an APK that `apksigner`
# reports as signed by any other certificate. Measuring another release adds a line.
#
# Where each value is published. KeePassDX writes an APK's hash into the text of its release, and
# the digest of its signing certificate into its README, under "Package authenticity from GitHub".
# GitHub reports a digest for the Keepass2Android 1.15-r6 asset and for the KeePassDroid 2.7 asset.
# Keepass2Android 1.08d-r5 has no published hash: that line is the hash of the file that arrived.
# No published digest was found for Keepass2Android's or KeePassDroid's signing certificate, so
# those two are what `apksigner` printed for these files, and what they buy is that both
# Keepass2Android releases carry the same certificate and that a later download is held to it.
#
# The last line is the command-line tools, which `sdk-setup.sh` fetches and checks itself. Its hash
# is the value on https://developer.android.com/studio, and a ZIP has no signer, hence the `-`.
